<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Javanese War of Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1749">1749</a>–<a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Javanese War of Succession was fought from 1749 to 1757 in the Mataram Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-iii">Pakubuwono III</a>, supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, and the rebel princes Mangkubumi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Raden Mas Said</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> (1755) divided Mataram between Surakarta and the new Sultanate of Yogyakarta under Mangkubumi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-salatiga">Treaty of Salatiga</a> (1757) created the Mangkunegaran.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Prince Mangkubumi</a> was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a> of Surakarta. One cause of the war was his humiliation by the Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, van Imhoff, who demanded the lease of the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> for 20,000 reales a year. Van Imhoff and the Patih Pringgalaya then persuaded Pakubuwana II to withdraw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Sukawati, now Sragen, promised to Mangkubumi for defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangkunegara-i">Raden Mas Said</a>'s rebellion there in 1746. Mangkubumi left Surakarta, rose against the Company and the king, and joined forces with his nephew and former enemy Raden Mas Said.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1749, in the midst of the rising, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwono-ii">Pakubuwana II</a> fell ill and asked Johan Andries, Baron van Hohendorff, to take control of the kingdom. Van Hohendorff asked for written confirmation, and on 11 December 1749 the king signed an agreement handing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of Mataram to the Company, which placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a> on the throne as a powerless ruler. Mangkubumi, who saw himself as the rightful heir, claimed the title of ruler, and the people of Mataram called him Hamengkubuwono.</p><p>Years of fighting followed, with battles at Grobogan and Demak. The turning point came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces under de Clerck suffered a heavy defeat at the Bogowonto River, after which Governor-General Mossel opened peace talks with Hamengkubuwono.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 1755 took its name from the place east of Surakarta where it was negotiated. The old capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, which years of war between brothers had made to seem doomed, was abandoned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-iii">Pakubuwana III</a> kept the eastern part of Mataram and built a new kraton in Surakarta, while Mangkubumi became Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-i">Hamengkubuwono I</a> of the western half and built his kraton at Yogyakarta, north of Kartasura. Raden Mas Said ruled the princely state of Mangkunegara as Mangkunegara I from a kraton near Surakarta. Mataram, once a single kingdom, was thus divided into three nominally independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Javanese War of Succession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
